1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
 Hello and welcome. This week we are going to be talking about conditioning and learning.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,000
 There's a whole bunch of really good YouTube videos out there and I have marked those for you to see.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 Some of those are going to happen within this lecture meeting as I have some numbers associated with certain slides.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 And you might want to stop the presentation, go watch the video and come back.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 They're not required, none of the information on the videos will be on the test,

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:35,000
 but they quite often allow you to see the material in a different way and to understand the material perhaps with a different voice.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 So let's start with talking about the different types of learning and the definition of learning is one that you do need to kind of understand.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,000
 And what we say is a relatively permanent change in behavior due to an experience.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 Now the reason we have this definition is that we don't consider learning to be something that you just do one time.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:02,000
 And it is based on experience, so if I'm a little kid and I've been told that the stove is hot, not the touch the stove when it's hot,

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 I keep wanting to go up and touch the stove. In fact, I've always got my hands all over the stove.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
 It's not working right now, but I've got my hand all over the stove.

13
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 Then I keep saying don't touch the stove because it's hot.

14
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:14,000
 And then one time I go over and I touch the stove and it's hot.

15
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 I learn not to touch the stove anymore because it's hot and I understand the difference.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,000
 So we now have a relatively permanent change in behavior as I get to be an adult.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 I'll know when to and when not to touch the stove, but I've changed because of this experience that I had.

18
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 So we say learning is this change that occurs.

19
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 Now there are two types of learning.

20
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:41,000
 A associative learning is what we'll be focusing on in this lecture, but there's also cognitive learning.

21
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 Now cognitive learning is what you do in school basically.

22
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
 It is what you learn in your brain you might want to say.

23
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,000
 So when we talk about learning math, that's cognitive learning.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,000
 We say learning directions, that's cognitive learning.

25
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 So cognitive learning is sort of what most of you think of as education or intelligence.

26
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
 But associative learning is very strong with us, and that's going to be classical and operant conditioning,

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
 and we're going to be talking about those a great deal here in a few minutes.

28
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 And basically, these are all based on stimuluses and responses.

29
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,000
 If you don't know what a stimulus is, a stimulus is anything that gets a response.

30
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 So I'm not going to do it because we're in a lecture right now, but if I was to suddenly scream in the classroom

31
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
 and you jumped, the scream would be the stimulus.

32
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
 The response would be the jump.

33
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
 With associative learning, there are a couple of big keywords that we want to understand.

34
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
 The first one is reinforcement.

35
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:51,000
 Now what I want to put for you to put right next to the word reinforcement is an increase in response,

36
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 or an increase in behavior.

37
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:59,000
 We always see an increase in behavior with reinforcement.

38
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
 I don't care what word goes in front of the word reinforcement.

39
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 If you see the word reinforcement, there is an increase in behavior.

40
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 So if we talk about positive reinforcement, we're talking about an increase in behavior.

41
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
 If I talk about negative reinforcement, I'm talking about an increase in behavior.

42
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:21,000
 If I talk about walk-a-walk-a reinforcement, there's still an increase in behavior.

43
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:30,000
 The other two words that are associated with associative learning, I was trying to think of a different word,

44
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,000
 is antecedents.

45
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 And antecedents are things that happen before a behavior and consequences, which are things that happen after a behavior.

46
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:46,000
 So I always think of associative behaviors as sort of the ABC of learning.

47
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 Now the antecedent is before the behavior and the consequence is after the behavior.

48
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
 Now antecedents are linked to classical conditioning.

49
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:59,000
 So when we start talking about classical conditioning, we're going to talk about something that happens before behavior,

50
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:02,000
 and thus it changes our behavior.

51
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
 With operant conditioning, we're going to talk about the behavior and then the consequence of that behavior,

52
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
 and how that changes the next time we do that behavior.

53
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:14,000
 So classical is before operant is after.

54
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 Let's again review, so you really understand the difference between these,

55
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:20,000
 because we're going to talk a lot about classical and operant.

56
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,000
 So classical is all about the antecedent and then the stimulus.

57
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
 So here's our antecedent and then we have this response.

58
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
 And the other thing about classical conditioning, it's all about reflexes.

59
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:37,000
 So what it is is it's all about the things that we do naturally, that we aren't trained to do.

60
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:44,000
 So blinking our eyes or sweating of our palms, they all come from the autotomic nervous system.

61
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:49,000
 Whereas operant conditioning is always going to be from the other nervous system.

62
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,000
 If it's an autotomic nervous system item, it's going to be classically conditioned.

63
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:56,000
 Everything else is going to come from operant conditioning.

64
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
 So it's all going to be about what happened after we do a certain thing.

65
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:08,000
 So if I make a funny joke and then you laugh, I decide, okay, I'm going to make a funny joke again because that worked.

66
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:11,000
 So that's operant conditioning, I made that decision.

67
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 But if I see a scary little spider and my palms begin to sweat, I can't make my palms sweat unless I try to heat them up or I do something.

68
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:28,000
 So that's an autotomic nervous system that means it's going to be classically conditioned.

69
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:36,000
 A classical conditioning first, and the big wig with classical conditioning is Pavlov.

70
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,000
 Now Pavlov is a pretty brilliant man. He actually wasn't looking for classical conditioning.

71
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:44,000
 He was sitting digestion in dogs.

72
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:51,000
 Now if you'd like to see the actual experiment, Mr. Pavlov and his dogs, if you go down to this YouTube video, they do have some real life footage.

73
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:59,000
 It's not a very long video, and again, it's number one underneath that list of YouTube videos for you to watch.

74
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:06,000
 But what Pavlov was doing was trying to find out why we digestion. What is the steps in digestion?

75
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 Well, the first step in digestion is to chew and make saliva, and the saliva helps break down the food.

76
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:20,000
 As such, it's very important for him when he rigs up these dogs to go ahead and have this little saliva collection.

77
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:26,000
 So he would present food the dogs would begin to chew and salivate or drool, and this drool would be caught.

78
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:29,000
 He'd measure how much drool they had.

79
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:33,000
 The only thing is, is think about this right now. I want you to drool.

80
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:39,000
 But you can't think about anything that tastes good and you can't bite your tongue.

81
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
 Yeah, it's kind of hard to do, isn't it? Because it's an autotomic nervous system.

82
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,000
 I don't have a stimulus, thus I'm not going to start to drool.

83
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:56,000
 Well, what happened with Pavlov, and what was very interesting, was that these dogs, when presented with food, would take a bite and they'd begin to drool.

84
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:04,000
 But after a bit of time, the dogs would begin to drool as they were being led to the area where they're going to be doing their experiments.

85
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:12,000
 And then after that, what began to happen is the dogs would begin to drool when the keepers would just begin to walk into the kennels.

86
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:17,000
 Well, it could be almost half an hour before they were actually going to be in the experiment.

87
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:23,000
 And so Pavlov was saying, "Well, how can these dogs begin to drool when they don't have a stimulus in front of them?"

88
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:33,000
 There's nothing to stimulate the creation of drool, which means they had to learn that this person who walked in was associated with food,

89
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:41,000
 and that that association is what created the dog's autotomic nervous system to begin to drool.

90
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:46,000
 Well, this is great, but remember, this is science, and it's got to be proven.

91
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:52,000
 So, what Pavlov does is begin to run a series of experiments.

92
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,000
 Now, he comes up with a few words, and we need to make sure we understand these Pavlovian terms as we move forward.

93
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,000
 The first one is a neutral stimulus, and that's basically a stimulus that does not evoke the desired response.

94
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:11,000
 I like to put the word "desired" response in there because it may evoke a response, but not the one that we're specifically looking for.

95
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:19,000
 A conditioned stimulus is one that evokes a response because we have taught the person to have that response to it.

96
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,000
 An unconditioned stimulus is one where, if presented the stimulus, you didn't have to be taught to do this.

97
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:31,000
 So, think about the dog and the food. It's presented some food. It naturally learns to drool.

98
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:35,000
 It didn't have to learn to create saliva when food is presented.

99
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:40,000
 So, stimulus is, if they're a correct stimulus, we'll get a response.

100
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,000
 An unconditioned response will be one in which we didn't have to learn it.

101
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:50,000
 So, we were given the unconditioned stimulus, and we started to drool.

102
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,000
 Whereas, a conditioned stimulus is going to lead to a conditioned response.

103
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:01,000
 Basically, what's going to happen is that I learned to respond this way.

104
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:08,000
 So, let's break this down a little bit easier. I kind of find sometimes the terminology by itself is a little looky.

105
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:13,000
 So, this is Pavlov and his experiment, and right here you see the same chart that's in your book,

106
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,000
 and we're basically going to do the same chart here, but I like using pictures.

107
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:22,000
 I find it, for me, a little bit more understandable.

108
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:25,000
 So, the first thing we're going to do is we're going to have this little bell,

109
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,000
 and we're going to be ringing the bell, and the dog will respond, but it doesn't drool.

110
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:37,000
 It doesn't make saliva. It may twist his head. It may bark, but basically this neutral stimulus will lead to nothing.

111
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:42,000
 Nothing means it doesn't give us the response we're looking for.

112
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:48,000
 So, now what we want to do is find this unconditioned stimulus to get this unconditioned response.

113
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:52,000
 So, if we give this dog meat, the dog is going to begin to drool to salivate.

114
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:56,000
 We know that the dog doesn't have to learn that. We don't have to learn that.

115
00:09:56,000 --> 00:10:01,000
 You put some good food in my mouth, and I begin to salivate to help the digestion process.

116
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:07,000
 Well, this is the first step. We have to prove that this bell right now does not get the desired response.

117
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:13,000
 Our hope is that in the end, we're going to be able to ring the bell over here, and that will get drool.

118
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:19,000
 So, how do we do that? Well, we need to build a relationship up between the bell and the food.

119
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:24,000
 So, basically, we're going to present the food and ring the bell exactly at the same time,

120
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:28,000
 and this relationship then is being built between these two things.

121
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:36,000
 This is called the acquisition phase, so we're acquiring the conditioning, because the food will naturally lead to drooling.

122
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:44,000
 But in the brain of the dog, within the autonomic nervous system, the food and the bell are becoming the same thing.

123
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
 The bell represents food, and food represents the bell.

124
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:54,000
 If we've done well, in the end, I ring the bell, and I get drool.

125
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:59,000
 Well, this neutral stimulus has changed to a conditioned stimulus, hasn't it?

126
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:04,000
 Because now I get a conditioned response, and that's one of the neat things about this,

127
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:10,000
 is that a neutral stimulus will lead to nothing, an unconditioned stimulus should always lead to an unconditioned response,

128
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:14,000
 and a conditioned stimulus should always lead to a conditioned response.

129
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:18,000
 Well, this pattern is a preset pattern. We don't have much choice with this.

130
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,000
 If we're talking about classical conditioning, this is the pattern.

131
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:28,000
 So, we can put anything that's been classically conditioned, put it into this pattern, and we should see it works the same way.

132
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:35,000
 So, let's talk about a five-year-old boy, Johnny. Johnny is in his backyard, and he sees a little garden snake.

133
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:41,000
 Now, Johnny's not afraid of the garden snake. He goes up, he's going to go touch it, or look at it, or do something with it, he doesn't know.

134
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:47,000
 So, right now, the snake is a neutral stimulus, because he does not produce fear when he sees the snake.

135
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:53,000
 However, as he goes to look at the snake, an 18-wheeler blows its horn.

136
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:59,000
 Now, if you have an 18-wheeler who blows a horn right behind you, you're probably going to jump or have fear.

137
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:02,000
 So, the horn produces fear.

138
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:08,000
 Now, what happened, unfortunately, is that he's looking at the snake at the same time the horn is sounded.

139
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:14,000
 So, he begins to associate fear, which was the response to the horn with the snake.

140
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:20,000
 And now, when Johnny goes in the backyard, he sees the snake. He begins to back up a little bit. His heart races a little bit.

141
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:25,000
 Now, he's a little fear of the snake. Now, in the beginning, he had no fear of the snake.

142
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:29,000
 Remember, he was all over going to go play with it and touch it. Now, he has a fear of the snake.

143
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:34,000
 So, that neutral stimulus has become the conditioned stimulus. It had to change.

144
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:47,000
 Now, this can happen in many different ways. We'll talk about some of the fun things in class, where we have seen some Pavlovian classically conditioned things to yourself.

145
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:52,000
 What is important is something called extinction. Now, here's our second one.

146
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:55,000
 This one's kind of funny. I really do recommend you find this one.

147
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,000
 You'll remember about Pavlovian as a log. He's from this video. It's a cute video.

148
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:07,000
 But extinction basically says what happens is that if this dog isn't given some meat powder every once in a while, when the bell is wrong,

149
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:16,000
 that after a while, that connection, that relationship I built up between the food and the bell will begin to dissolve.

150
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:21,000
 And so extinction can occur. So, just because something happens once doesn't mean that it will be there forever.

151
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:28,000
 Or if we don't keep having it repeated after a while, the response we have to it is not there.

152
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:35,000
 Spontaneous recovery basically says, okay, so I had the dog in the bell and I did it 15 times.

153
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:39,000
 And after the 15th time, the dog would begin to drool whenever he heard the bell.

154
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:47,000
 But then I ring the bell 20 different times and the dog stopped drooling after a while because he's like, you know what, you're not giving me any food when you ring the bell.

155
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:51,000
 I figure this one out. It does not link anymore. There's no relationship.

156
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:59,000
 But then only one time I give him some food or even smell food when I ring the bell, he can spontaneously recover that training that he's had.

157
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:08,000
 That's one of the things with classical conditioning is that they're very easy to begin to reestablish that conditioning once it's been done to somebody.

158
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:15,000
 Now, the other thing about classical conditioning is that this is a phobia.

159
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:18,000
 Now, the big thing with phobias was done with a little Albert.

160
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:23,000
 And if you want to watch about a little Albert you read about in your book, you can go over here to this YouTube video.

161
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:28,000
 It's number three on that list and actually see a little Albert in this experiment.

162
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:32,000
 And basically, a phobia is a little different than a fear.

163
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:41,000
 A fear is something that is natural. We should naturally be standing at the top of this building looking out and saying, that's a long way down.

164
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:48,000
 Our heart may race a little bit because we know that if we were to step off this building and go, we, we go splat.

165
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:55,000
 And most of us don't want to go splat. So we have a natural fear looking out over high things.

166
00:14:55,000 --> 00:15:01,000
 We know that danger is there. However, we still went up to the building.

167
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:10,000
 A phobia is when there's no real danger and it also may change our behavior.

168
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:18,000
 A phobia is an unrealistic fear. We can even know that the fear is wrong.

169
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:22,000
 And we know that we shouldn't be afraid. It doesn't mean that we're not afraid anyway.

170
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:28,000
 So while I might have a little fear standing up here looking out and a phobia, I couldn't get up in the first place.

171
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:31,000
 I'm standing down here and I'm saying, I am not taking an elevator up there.

172
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:35,000
 Nope. Nope. Not going up. Nope. Refuse.

173
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:42,000
 Now, the truth is I know I'm going to be inside this building and I know that I'm not going to fall out of the building by going up the elevator and just standing up there.

174
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:51,000
 But my fear, my unrealistic fear, that something would happen to me if I do that is so strong I can't even get in the elevator.

175
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:55,000
 That's when it becomes a phobia. That's when it crosses over.

176
00:15:55,000 --> 00:16:01,000
 So what do we know about phobias? Well, little Albert was a baby and he was just a normal little kid.

177
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:05,000
 And basically they had this little dog that he played with and he liked the little dog.

178
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:09,000
 The dog was very cheerful and he seemed to be very happy with the dog at first.

179
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:16,000
 The next time they presented the dog, the dog basically went into this barking play position and that scared little Albert.

180
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:22,000
 But it scared him very severely because we're talking about a little teeny brain here that doesn't have a lot of power in it.

181
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:32,000
 What is interesting though is that because a dog is sort of indistinguishable from a cat to a baby.

182
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
 Do they know the difference between a dog and a cat? No.

183
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:38,000
 They know a dog has got four legs, fur, and a tail.

184
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:43,000
 So this animal he's afraid of has got four legs, a tail, and has got fur.

185
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:46,000
 A cat has also got four legs, fur, and a tail.

186
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:52,000
 As a baby he doesn't know the difference between a cat and a dog. He just knows that it's got four legs, fur, and a tail.

187
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:56,000
 And I don't like things that have got four legs, fur, and a tail.

188
00:16:56,000 --> 00:17:03,000
 And cats can be a little aloof and maybe even sort of his set you a little bit and he's gone, boom, I'm out of there.

189
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:10,000
 So what will begin to happen now is that he will begin to associate fear with anything that's got four legs, fur, and a tail.

190
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:15,000
 So rabbits will become problematic. Llamas will become problematic.

191
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:21,000
 Basically animals may become fearful to him. He begins to develop a phobia of animals.

192
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:27,000
 It's an unrealistic fear. You can even know that it's unrealistic that that horse is two miles away.

193
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,000
 It doesn't matter. It's a big animal and I'm afraid of it.

194
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:41,000
 Phobias are interesting because you can experience a phobia by doing this, but we can also learn phobias vicariously through others.

195
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:50,000
 So if I watch somebody who's got a phobia, I can learn that from them, especially for a child.

196
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:55,000
 So I'm sorry. I'm going to pick on the ladies who go eee to those good old palmetto bugs.

197
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:02,000
 The palmetto bugs really don't want to be in your house, but they got washed out from outside. Their houses were destroyed or whatever we put poison down.

198
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:06,000
 They're like, fine, I'll move in here. And you see them?

199
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:10,000
 Well, the truth is that we should just take a big old shoe and whack it and kill it.

200
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:16,000
 But I'm a little girl. I watch my mother go eee and run away and dad comes over and goes whack with the shoe and he kills it.

201
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:23,000
 So the girl has begun to learn that I should be fearful of this bug because my mother was fearful of this bug.

202
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:30,000
 And that I shouldn't go near it because I'm vicariously learning through others.

203
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:39,000
 And so as she grows up, she may also start to develop a phobia of the palmetto bugs and eee and run away and can't do anything.

204
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:45,000
 So what we want to do is look at what are true phobias and what are fears.

205
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:52,000
 So remember, fears are realistic. We should be afraid to want a tall building and we're looking down. A little heart race is not a bad thing.

206
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:57,000
 It keeps us from basically doing harm to ourselves. That's what fear does.

207
00:18:57,000 --> 00:19:03,000
 A phobia is an unrealistic fear, one that we even know that maybe we shouldn't be afraid of, but our body still reacts.

208
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:11,000
 It still is stimulated to that fear. So if I'm saying I won't go up that elevator or even if I do go up the elevator, I begin to sweat profusely.

209
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:19,000
 My heart begins to pound. And I'm like, jeez, I shouldn't be afraid, but my body is reacting anyway. That then becomes a phobia.

210
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:24,000
 So I have a phobia. It took me a long time to realize it was a phobia.

211
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:29,000
 But what I found was, is I have lived most of my life here in Florida with no big problems.

212
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:36,000
 When I moved to the big city, I found that I just kept trying to get tall apartments.

213
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:42,000
 Or I'd only live in apartments that were three stories or two stories, and I'd always live on the second or third story.

214
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:49,000
 When I went to Baltimore, we started getting 10, 15 story apartment complexes, and I worked hard to move up as high as I could.

215
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:53,000
 I also began to discover that I didn't like being under bridges.

216
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:57,000
 And here in Florida, we have a lot of bridges, but I go over bridges.

217
00:19:57,000 --> 00:20:02,000
 I have no problem going over the Skyway Bridge, which is one of the highest bridges here in the state of Florida.

218
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:07,000
 No problem at being at the top of the bridge. What I don't like doing is being under the bridge.

219
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:13,000
 And so in Atlanta, there was an eight-lane highway, and I would get stopped every time underneath this bridge.

220
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:17,000
 And you'd look up, and the bridge would be slightly bouncing.

221
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:22,000
 Now, I know that that means that it's actually a healthy bridge. Bridges should flex, depending on the weather,

222
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:26,000
 especially in someplace where there could be ice. We want the ability to flex.

223
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:33,000
 But my heart would race. My palms would begin to sweat. I would begin to think about how do I get out of here.

224
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:40,000
 I got to the point where I began to find another way to get to work, so I could avoid that eight-lane highway,

225
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:43,000
 because you just never could seem to get all the way through without stopping at the light.

226
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:49,000
 So eventually I find a way around to work that doesn't take very long, and it gets me there, but I avoid the highway.

227
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:57,000
 Now, I've got a phobia, because I'm refusing to stay in low buildings. I'm refusing to drive one way.

228
00:20:57,000 --> 00:21:03,000
 I changed my life around. I even know this is kind of silly, but that's what happened.

229
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:08,000
 I also began to discover I didn't like caves. I couldn't go in caves.

230
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:14,000
 Sometimes these caves were huge and enormous and beautiful, but I just couldn't go in it.

231
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:21,000
 So the question is, is what do I have a phobia of? If you think it's claustrophobia, that's not right.

232
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:30,000
 Because remember, I had no problem going into being in a large cave. It was the cave part that scared me.

233
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:35,000
 What I actually have a fear of, a phobia, is being crushed.

234
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:46,000
 I just know if I go in that cave, even though that cave has been standing there for 3 million years, 3 million in one day will be the day that cave crashes in on me, and I will get crushed.

235
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:53,000
 I just know that if I'm in a low apartment complex, something happens, I'm going to get the one that gets crushed.

236
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:56,000
 If I'm underneath that bridge, it's going to fall down on me.

237
00:21:56,000 --> 00:22:01,000
 Now, have I ever been crushed? No, I've never been crushed.

238
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:07,000
 So the question is, how did I develop this phobia? Well, I developed it probably vicariously through other things.

239
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:16,000
 I watched videos of the San Francisco earthquake where we saw this two decker highway and one deck fell down on top of the other deck.

240
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:22,000
 Now, the people in the cars on the top deck, they didn't die. The people in the cars on the bottom deck, they got crushed.

241
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:27,000
 Things like Haiti don't help me much because there's lots of crushed people in those things.

242
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:31,000
 Hurricane Andrew, I see all these things where things are happening.

243
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:37,000
 So what happens is I originally have this phobia and it keeps getting reinforced all the time.

244
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:41,000
 So I never get to lose the phobia because it's reinforced.

245
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:52,000
 However, through desensitization, discussing this phobia in classes, I have gone to the point where my heart doesn't begin to raise and my palms don't begin to sweat.

246
00:22:52,000 --> 00:23:00,000
 It was funny when I first did these lectures, it was very hard for me to even do the lecture because my heart would pound and my palms begin to sweat.

247
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:09,000
 But as I begin to talk more about it, as I begin to realize I have more control over this, I gradually have gotten less and less fear to the stimulus.

248
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:19,000
 Now, I still have a little bit harder time staying on the first or second floor of a 10-story apartment complex, or especially when I go to hotels, I always like to ask to stay up higher up.

249
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:29,000
 But I have gotten to the point where, you know what, I don't stay up all night because I have this fear that the apartment complex is going to crash on me or the hotel is going to come crashing on me.

250
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:41,000
 I can now sleep, it's not the most comfortable sleep, but I can do that because I've desensitized myself some to this in realizing that there's a good chance that it won't happen today, you might want to say.

251
00:23:41,000 --> 00:23:53,000
 It's one reason I probably can never live in California, the earthquakes drive me nuts too much, but it's an unrealistic fear, thus it is a phobia.

252
00:23:53,000 --> 00:24:04,000
 Now, the next type of condition we're going to talk about is operant conditioning, and operant conditioning is all about learning a response to a consequence.

253
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:16,000
 So basically, I have a behavior, and then I look to see how do you respond to that. Now, the big person with operant conditioning is Skinner, and Skinner did a lot of work with pigeons and mice.

254
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:24,000
 And Skinner, B.F. Skinner is our biggest American, probably psychologist that we can think of in America.

255
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:29,000
 This video is a little bit slow, but it is kind of interesting for those who like this kind of stuff.

256
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:37,000
 You actually get to see him train pigeons, he thought pigeons were far more intelligent than mice, and so he stopped working with mice after a while and started working with pigeons.

257
00:24:37,000 --> 00:24:51,000
 You can see this also in your book, you'll see a picture of two pigeons playing ping pong, and he trained them to play ping pong, because the one who got the ball past the other one would get some food, and so they like food, so they would work hard to do that.

258
00:24:51,000 --> 00:25:00,000
 Now, if you're looking at this page, you also notice a couple of things, one is called the law of effects, and one of the things you may have realized is by now, we don't talk about a lot of laws here in psychology.

259
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:09,000
 Remember, psychology is a science, and science is generally of laws. Why don't we talk about laws? Well, the primary reason is that laws mean that something doesn't change.

260
00:25:09,000 --> 00:25:16,000
 It always stays the same, but we're talking about psychology here. The human body is changing, the brain is changing.

261
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:22,000
 So we don't have too many laws here in psychology because things are changing, so we tend to have more theories and hypothesis.

262
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:36,000
 Well, the law of effects basically says that if this response alters something, I'm going to be likely to change my response each time, so this is all about that behavior and thus the consequence.

263
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:48,000
 So we talk about reinforcers, and we talk about punishers. So remember, reinforcement was an increase in behavior, and punishment is going to be a decrease in behavior.

264
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:57,000
 So we have two types of reinforcement. We have positive reinforcement and negative reinforcement, and positive reinforcement is basically a positive event, begins.

265
00:25:57,000 --> 00:26:04,000
 So we think about this little girl, she comes home, she does her little dance to her mom and dad, and they give her clap, clap, clap, clap, clap.

266
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:12,000
 What's she going to do next week? Oh, she's going to come home. She's going to say, "Mom and dad, look at me. Look at me. I've learned this new dance at school, and they're going to go, oh, do the dance."

267
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:22,000
 She does the dance. They go, clap, clap, clap, clap, clap, clap, clap. And she does this over three or four weeks. She keeps coming home, and she's asking mom and dad to watch her dance, and each time she does the dance, they get the clap, clap, clap, clap, clap, clap.

268
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:27,000
 So on week five, she comes from running home, and she just starts dancing for them.

269
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:37,000
 So what's changed? Well, she stopped asking. She stopped asking because for the last five weeks, every time she did a dance, she got a positive event that started right, clap, clap, clap, clap, clap, clap, clap.

270
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:44,000
 So why wouldn't she dance this week? Of course she would. She's learned that this is a good thing.

271
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:52,000
 So she's got an evil twin sister, and the evil twin sister, of course, is too delicate for ballet. She's going to be a soccer player.

272
00:26:52,000 --> 00:27:03,000
 And mom and dad have been pretty smart. After all, they got somebody else to do the ballet. They've got somebody else to take their evil twin daughter to soccer practice.

273
00:27:03,000 --> 00:27:12,000
 And so the daughter comes home from soccer practice at the very first stage. She comes running in the door with the ball, and she says, "Mommy, daddy, mommy, daddy, let me show you what I've learned today in soccer."

274
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:25,000
 And mom says, "Not in the house. You take the ball outside. We don't have balls in the house." And dad doesn't quite look over, and so she puts the ball down on the ground, and she kicks it anyway, and crash. It hits a vase.

275
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:32,000
 Dad kind of looks at and says, "Go to your room. Your mom told you not to kick that ball in the house, and you kick the ball anyway. Go to your room."

276
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:35,000
 And so she pouts, and off she goes to the room.

277
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:45,000
 Well week two comes along, and she comes running in from soccer practice, and she says, "Mommy, daddy, mommy, daddy, I learned something new today. I learned how to kind of spin and kick at the same time."

278
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:51,000
 And mom looks over at her and says, "What did I tell you last week? You take that ball out of the house."

279
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:55,000
 And the little girl looks at her and looks at dad, dad said, "You hurt her."

280
00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:00,000
 And she spins and kicks the ball anyway, and crash this time, and lamp breaks.

281
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
 Oh, this really gets the parents upset. "You go to your room. We told you you couldn't do this."

282
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:13,000
 And the girl starts saying things like, "Oh, you don't like me. You like my sister better. You do all the things for her."

283
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:20,000
 "Oh, don't talk like that. You know we love you just as much. Yeah, I don't want to go to my room. That's not fair."

284
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:27,000
 And on and on this little argument goes for a couple of minutes, and eventually a girl goes off to her room.

285
00:28:27,000 --> 00:28:32,000
 I think she's learned her lesson. Well, let's see what happens on week three.

286
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:37,000
 Week three comes in. She runs in the house. She says, "Watch. I've learned how to do a backward kick."

287
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:42,000
 She put the ball down on the ground, and just before she kicks the ball, the mom grabs it and says,

288
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:51,000
 "I have told you. You will not kick this ball in this house. You will not go to soccer anymore. You are out of this."

289
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:58,000
 And the girl bursts into tears and says, "You don't love me. Me, me, me, me, me, me, me, me, me."

290
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:06,000
 And stops off to her room. Well, the question is, is why did the girl want to kick the ball in the house again?

291
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:14,000
 Wasn't she punished each time? Well, the answer is no. She actually wasn't punished each time.

292
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:22,000
 You see, but the parents yelled at her. Ah, but you see, you're looking at things based on being a teacher.

293
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:29,000
 Are we concerned about the teacher or the learner? In this case, the little girl is the learner and the parents are the teacher.

294
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:34,000
 What is it that the learner wants from this exchange? What is the learner looking to get?

295
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:38,000
 And the learner is looking to get attention.

296
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:45,000
 Now, we've already called her the evil twin sister, so we already know she's kind of defined herself as the bad girl.

297
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:51,000
 But the thing is, is the first time she came running in the house with the ball, her parents wanted to send her outside.

298
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:57,000
 Was she going to get attention outside? No, because they didn't say they'd come with her. They said, "You go outside."

299
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:00,000
 So that was actually punishing her. She wasn't going to get attention.

300
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:05,000
 So if she wasn't going to get attention that way, she was going to get attention another way.

301
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:10,000
 And so she kicked the ball anyway. They paid attention to her by yelling at her and throwing her in the room.

302
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:14,000
 The next week comes by. She comes running in and again.

303
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:20,000
 They don't offer to take her outside. They just simply say, "Stop. Don't do this." She's not getting attention.

304
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:24,000
 So she goes ahead and kicks the ball anyway. She breaks the lamp.

305
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:28,000
 This time she gets a lot of attention, doesn't she? Because there's all kinds of arguments back and forth.

306
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:31,000
 She's getting what she wants, which is attention.

307
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:35,000
 Now you may say, "But I'm still punishing. It's not what she wants." No, all she wants is attention.

308
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:39,000
 If she's not going to get attention one way, she's going to try to get attention another way.

309
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:42,000
 And after all, she's already defined herself as the evil twin sister.

310
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:45,000
 So she knows the only way she's going to get attention is by being bad.

311
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:51,000
 And the parents are falling right into it. So the parents are actually giving her positive reinforcement.

312
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:57,000
 Why? Because a positive event has started for her. She wanted attention and she's getting attention.

313
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:04,000
 So in their own way, the parents may not realize this, but they are actually reinforcing her behavior to act badly.

314
00:31:04,000 --> 00:31:07,000
 And this is a very common mistake that we find that people do.

315
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:11,000
 They don't realize that they're actually positive reinforcing bad events.

316
00:31:11,000 --> 00:31:17,000
 Because what the person wants the most, what this child wants, is attention.

317
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:22,000
 She is no different with her soccer ball than her sister is with the dancing.

318
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:28,000
 They both want attention. They both got attention. So then what the heck is negative reinforcement?

319
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:31,000
 Well, negative reinforcement only takes something bad away.

320
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:35,000
 We like when you take bad things away. So let's think about this way.

321
00:31:35,000 --> 00:31:38,000
 I've got a headache. A headache is not a good thing, is it?

322
00:31:38,000 --> 00:31:43,000
 I don't like a headache. I don't want a headache. I want headaches to go away as fast as possible.

323
00:31:43,000 --> 00:31:48,000
 As such, I take Ms. Birmingham's magical elixir. And what do you know, and about?

324
00:31:48,000 --> 00:31:53,000
 Oh, a minute. My headache is gone. I feel wonderful.

325
00:31:53,000 --> 00:32:00,000
 So I had this bad thing I didn't like. I took this medication and it ended my discomfort.

326
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:05,000
 So what am I going to do next time? I'm discomforted. Next time I've got a headache.

327
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:09,000
 I'm going to go find Ms. Birmingham's magical elixir, aren't I? And I'm going to take it again.

328
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:12,000
 Because it's going to get rid of my headache very quickly.

329
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:18,000
 Well, the other way we can look at this, especially with adults, is anxiety.

330
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:23,000
 Most of us do not like anxiety. We don't particularly like feeling bad.

331
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:29,000
 And again, we can also kind of realize that for most of us, if we go to work, we don't go to work to do a bad job.

332
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:35,000
 We try to go to work to do a good job. And so let's say I'm at work and I'm a cashier.

333
00:32:35,000 --> 00:32:39,000
 And I begin to get this feeling that, I don't know, something is wrong.

334
00:32:39,000 --> 00:32:43,000
 I think my cash drawer is off. I think this guy ripped me off.

335
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:52,000
 Well, I've got anxiety now, don't I? I've got this little nagging feeling that something is wrong and that I've been sort of played.

336
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:57,000
 Most of us don't like being played. So I've got this anxiety. I don't like this anxiety.

337
00:32:57,000 --> 00:33:01,000
 And now I've got to go and tell a boss. I've got two different bosses.

338
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:06,000
 I've got the one. If I go tell them, they're going to yell and scream at me and tell me I'm an idiot kind of thing.

339
00:33:06,000 --> 00:33:11,000
 And how do I let myself get ripped off? Well, that's only going to increase my anxiety, aren't I?

340
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:13,000
 So I'm probably not going to go to them.

341
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:16,000
 Now, I've got the other boss who's probably going to help me count my cash drawer.

342
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:20,000
 He's maybe going to go back over the videotape and let's see if we can find out what happened.

343
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:29,000
 I may get a little little lecture at the end of this because, well, the truth is I probably deserve a little lecture on how to be safer with the cash and when people are handing me cash.

344
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:32,000
 But that person's going to get rid of my anxiety, aren't they?

345
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:36,000
 I don't like anxiety. They're going to get rid of my anxiety.

346
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:41,000
 So what am I going to do the next time I'm not feeling comfortable in the store or I think something wrong has gone on?

347
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:44,000
 I'm going to go find the manager who helped me get rid of my anxiety.

348
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:48,000
 In fact, I may become very loyal to that manager because that manager helps me.

349
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:57,000
 And because they get rid of my anxiety, which is a bad thing, they are using negative reinforcement to increase the behavior that I'm going to report things to them.

350
00:33:57,000 --> 00:34:02,000
 So they're going to get to know everything that's going on where the other manager has no idea what's going on.

351
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:09,000
 So negative reinforcement is something that's still seen all the time and something that we still respond to on a day basis.

352
00:34:09,000 --> 00:34:17,000
 Again, I've got a really funny, I think it's kind of funny, a YouTube video about positive and negative reinforcement that you can watch.

353
00:34:17,000 --> 00:34:22,000
 Now the timing of reinforcement becomes really critical.

354
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:32,000
 Now think about it, if that little girl came home and she did a little dance, but we waited, I don't know, an hour or two later to tell her what good job she did, it would be too long.

355
00:34:32,000 --> 00:34:35,000
 It would be too far afterwards, especially for a little kid.

356
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:40,000
 Now what most child psychologists will say is that kids can only wait one minute per age of life.

357
00:34:40,000 --> 00:34:43,000
 And basically, if they're under three, they're not waiting at all.

358
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:49,000
 So if I'm five years old, the best I can do is wait five minutes if I've got good patience at all.

359
00:34:49,000 --> 00:34:54,000
 But for most kids, less than five minutes is what they're going to be able to wait at five years old.

360
00:34:54,000 --> 00:34:59,000
 But it is through the timing of this reinforcement that we can begin to shape behavior.

361
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:08,000
 So let's say that I do have this, oh, I've got a kid who's not very good at getting up in the morning.

362
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:10,000
 And so what I want to do is begin to shape their behavior.

363
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:14,000
 I want them to brush their teeth, wash their face and make their bed.

364
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:19,000
 If I ask them to do all three things right away, there's a good chance that they're not going to get it done.

365
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:26,000
 So what I'm going to do is spend a little time just basically saying, look, we want you to get up in the morning and to just throw your cover over the bed.

366
00:35:26,000 --> 00:35:38,000
 And as I see that they're doing this and they're being able to start doing this without being told, I'm telling them that they're doing a good job and maybe they even get a reward if they can do it five days in a row without me telling them they get to go to the dollar store or something.

367
00:35:38,000 --> 00:35:48,000
 Well, now that they've got that behavior down, now I say, okay, you know what, you're doing so good on that, you go ahead and do that, but we also want you to begin to brush your teeth right after you make your bed.

368
00:35:48,000 --> 00:36:00,000
 And so it may take me a little while to get them to make their bed and then brush their teeth, but after a while they're doing that without me having to tell them too much to do it, just say, hey, time to go get ready, they go make their bed and brush their teeth.

369
00:36:00,000 --> 00:36:12,000
 Now I want them to brush their teeth and then I want them to wash their face. So each time what I'm doing is I'm adding a new behavior once they've got the other behaviors basically molded and ready to do.

370
00:36:12,000 --> 00:36:26,000
 Now, the reason that shaping is important is that what it tells us that we have to do this by a step-by-step process, operant conditioning doesn't just occur in a one-time step, it generally takes multiple times and multiple small steps to get there.

371
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:40,000
 Well, one of the results of operant conditioning could also be superstitious behavior. So here we have this baseball player, and if you've ever tried to play baseball, hitting a round ball with a round bat, not a very easy thing to do.

372
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:49,000
 And so let's say we have this little baseball player here, he's kind of a nice kid, he's not the best player in the world, he's not the worst player in the world, he's just a nice medium player.

373
00:36:49,000 --> 00:37:02,000
 Now, he sometimes hits, sometimes doesn't hit, but this particular game he gets there, and he does great, absolutely fabulous. He's never hit the ball as well.

374
00:37:02,000 --> 00:37:16,000
 And he's trying to figure out what was different today than any other day, and he says, "Well, you know, I don't know, same bat, same ball, same me, same pitcher I've gone up against him before, but you don't have these new socks on." That's the only thing that was different.

375
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:22,000
 Now, the first time it doesn't really maybe connect, but you know, they kind of realize they have these new socks on.

376
00:37:22,000 --> 00:37:33,000
 They go to practice the next day, and of course they don't have their new socks on, because they're dirty, mom's going to wash them, but they go and practice, and you know, they don't do as well.

377
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:43,000
 Don't, again, think much about it. But the day after that, the socks are washed, they happen to have them on, they go to practice, and they're just hitting really good, and they go, "Wow, look at this, I'm hitting good again."

378
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:48,000
 So what was the same today as two days ago when I was at the game?

379
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:52,000
 The only thing they can think that's the same is these socks.

380
00:37:52,000 --> 00:37:58,000
 So they say, "Well, maybe the socks are lucky." Yeah, yeah, lucky socks, that's what it is.

381
00:37:58,000 --> 00:38:05,000
 So the next day, they decide to wear their lucky socks again, and guess what? They bat really well.

382
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:14,000
 Well, now we've got some superstitious behavior beginning to occur. The truth is that socks do not make you a better baseball player.

383
00:38:14,000 --> 00:38:25,000
 However, if they begin to believe that the socks will make them a better baseball player, then they're going to feel more confident when they have the socks on, and the more confident they feel, the better of a baseball player they're going to be.

384
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:36,000
 That's a self-fulfilling prophecy that we talked about in chapter one. So because I believe that the socks are going to help me, I become more confident, and the more confident I become, the better player I become.

385
00:38:36,000 --> 00:38:41,000
 So when I don't have the socks, I get anxiety, and when I'm anxious, I don't hit as well.

386
00:38:41,000 --> 00:38:45,000
 So the socks do become sort of a good-look charm you might want to say.

387
00:38:45,000 --> 00:38:49,000
 Well, again, we have to prove this through science, and how did we actually see this in science?

388
00:38:49,000 --> 00:38:59,000
 Well, this is Skinner again, and if you're looking at your chapter and you're looking at these different things, you saw a little picture of a mouse in a box, and that mouse in the box is called the Skinner box.

389
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:06,000
 Now, what we want the mouse to do is to touch that little rod, and when you touch the rod, a piece of food drops out, and the mouse gets a reward.

390
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:18,000
 Well, the one thing about mice is that they are very curious. They touch and feel everything so they can kind of know their whereabouts, and their whiskers are actually sensing organs for them. They can sense a lot with their whiskers.

391
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:24,000
 As such, they like to keep their whiskers clean. Just so like cats keep their whiskers clean a lot.

392
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:33,000
 So a mouse, let's say, cleans his whiskers, and right after he cleans his whiskers, for the first time, he touches the metal bar, and there comes a piece of food.

393
00:39:33,000 --> 00:39:42,000
 Well, yum, yum, yum, yum. Eat the piece of food. My whiskers are dirty. I clean the whiskers. I am still hungry. I touch the bar. Let's see if it happens again, and boom!

394
00:39:42,000 --> 00:39:45,000
 Well, what do you know? Another piece of food.

395
00:39:45,000 --> 00:39:49,000
 Now, the only thing the mouse has to do is actually touch the bar.

396
00:39:49,000 --> 00:39:55,000
 However, what's happened is the mouse has begun to associate, cleaning the whiskers, touch the bar, get the food.

397
00:39:55,000 --> 00:40:01,000
 And you can see these mice begin to clean their whiskers so quickly. They go one, two, three, boom!

398
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:05,000
 And touch the bar, get food. One, two, three, touch the bar, get food.

399
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:16,000
 The truth is that they have picked up a superstitious behavior, because to them in their brain, the two behaviors are linked in order to get the consequence that they desire.

400
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:28,000
 For a nice little review about operant conditioning, I do recommend this YouTube video. You might want to wait until we get to the end on all of these things, but it is a nice little kind of review of all this.

401
00:40:28,000 --> 00:40:35,000
 We talked about reinforcement. Let's talk about punishment. And punishment is any time we have an adverse consequence.

402
00:40:35,000 --> 00:40:42,000
 Basically, a good thing stops or a bad thing starts. So punishment, a good thing stops, a bad thing starts.

403
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:47,000
 And we talk about two types of punishment, severe punishment and mild punishment.

404
00:40:47,000 --> 00:40:55,000
 Now, the more severe the punishment is, the more likely that we're not going to see a long-term change in behavior.

405
00:40:55,000 --> 00:40:58,000
 We may see a short-term change in behavior, but not a long-term.

406
00:40:58,000 --> 00:41:07,000
 The more mild the punishment, mild to medium punishment, the more likely when linked with other items, we're going to see a longer-term change.

407
00:41:07,000 --> 00:41:13,000
 So let's talk about punishment. I'm going 95 on 95, because after all, that's what it says, right? 95.

408
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:20,000
 And shortly I see those lights in my back rear mirror and he pulls me over.

409
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:26,000
 And I'm on 95, and because I'm going 95 miles an hour, he gives me a $300 speeding ticket.

410
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:33,000
 That's pretty severe, isn't it? The question is, is how long is it before you start to speed again?

411
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:40,000
 The truth is, some of you, you said as soon as the lights are out of my rear view, man, I'm speeding again.

412
00:41:40,000 --> 00:41:49,000
 Others of you said, well, it may be a little while, maybe after I pay off the $300 ticket or a week or two, but yeah, my heavy foot is going to just keep me going down.

413
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:56,000
 Well, that was severe punishment, and for most of us, it really didn't work to make long-term change of behavior, did it?

414
00:41:56,000 --> 00:42:07,000
 But then, there are those areas in Brevard County, and I happen to live near one here in Indiana, where you know if you go even a mile over the speed limit, you're getting a ticket.

415
00:42:07,000 --> 00:42:16,000
 We have an area here in Indiana, where it's a 25-mile-an-hour speed zone, and it's very rare you see somebody go more than 27 or 28 miles an hour.

416
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:21,000
 Why? Because the cops are always there, and they're constantly giving tickets.

417
00:42:21,000 --> 00:42:29,000
 Now, they don't give $300 tickets, these tend to be more like 75 to $100 tickets, they're much milder, but we get them a lot more often.

418
00:42:29,000 --> 00:42:41,000
 So you very quickly realize that, you know, I'm not going to be able to get away with this, and it's not worth this aggravation of getting these little tickets all the time, because the little tickets add up, but it's a week or punishment, but it's done more often.

419
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:47,000
 So it's the fact that it tends to do things more often that we see this change begin to occur.

420
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:57,000
 Now, it may be a little longer for the change to occur, but it's going to be for a longer period of time, so that's over a medium punishment.

421
00:42:57,000 --> 00:43:07,000
 Now, a very weak punishment, one that is just talky-talky-talky, sort of I'm going to throw you out of this park, I'm going to do this, I'm going to do that, but you don't follow through.

422
00:43:07,000 --> 00:43:14,000
 We see a very slow response, and it's a very temporary response, because we realize that you're not going to follow through on it.

423
00:43:14,000 --> 00:43:24,000
 So with punishment, it is going to be the severity of the punishment and the consistency of the punishment on whether or not it's going to have an absolute change in behavior.

424
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:36,000
 One of the big downsides to punishment is that if the punishment is severe, what we're going to find is that we're going to have an adverse stimulus.

425
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:46,000
 Basically, let's put this way. If I punish too hard, all I'm going to do is either escape the punishment or I'm going to avoid the punishment.

426
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:57,000
 So let's say that I'm a kid who gets bullied at school, made fun of. I go home and I tell my parents, "I can't stay in school anymore, they're yelling at me, they're making fun of me."

427
00:43:57,000 --> 00:44:00,000
 And your parents say, "Well, you've got to go to school anyway."

428
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:06,000
 I'm just going to start skipping school, aren't I? Because I'm going to want to avoid or escape what's happening to me.

429
00:44:06,000 --> 00:44:11,000
 If I'm a kid who's been told that if I drink and drive, I'll never be allowed out again in my life.

430
00:44:11,000 --> 00:44:23,000
 And now, all of a sudden, I've sort of slipped up, I've had a couple of beers, I'm feeling a little fuzzy, but I'm afraid to tell them I'm going to go drive home myself anyway because I'm afraid of what's going to happen.

431
00:44:23,000 --> 00:44:30,000
 I have avoidance of trying to get punished and I wind up in an accident because I really shouldn't have been drinking.

432
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:35,000
 I was so afraid of the punishment that I was willing to take a risky behavior.

433
00:44:35,000 --> 00:44:43,000
 So we have to be careful with punishment that it needs to be medium to mild and that it needs to actually be directed at the action.

434
00:44:43,000 --> 00:44:50,000
 But punish only, we're not going to see a change in behavior.

435
00:44:50,000 --> 00:44:56,000
 What this little chart is doing is sort of looking at all the different things about punishment and reinforcement.

436
00:44:56,000 --> 00:45:02,000
 What we want is to have basically reinforcement and punishment happening at the same time.

437
00:45:02,000 --> 00:45:16,000
 So we have to remember that both positive reinforcement and negative reinforcement lead to an increase in behavior and that punishment, whether we're taking away something that's good or we're starting something that's bad,

438
00:45:16,000 --> 00:45:20,000
 is supposedly going to lead to a decrease in behavior.

439
00:45:20,000 --> 00:45:26,000
 Well, if we want the fastest change in behavior, we want a combination of both.

440
00:45:26,000 --> 00:45:31,000
 We want to reinforce a good behavior and punish the bad behaviors.

441
00:45:31,000 --> 00:45:35,000
 So let's say I have a 10-year-old who loves to interrupt me when I'm on the phone.

442
00:45:35,000 --> 00:45:37,000
 Now, sorry, I can't do much with the little kids.

443
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:40,000
 As soon as they hear you talk, they're stimulated to talk to you.

444
00:45:40,000 --> 00:45:48,000
 So you're on the phone and a five-year-old is going to want to talk to you at the same time because they hear a voice and it says, "Oh, time for me to talk to."

445
00:45:48,000 --> 00:45:49,000
 But let's use the 10-year-old.

446
00:45:49,000 --> 00:45:55,000
 The 10-year-olds should be old enough to begin to understand that when I'm on the phone, it's not the most appropriate time to talk to me.

447
00:45:55,000 --> 00:46:04,000
 So let's say I make a phone call and at the end of the phone call, I've been on for half an hour and the kid hasn't interrupted me at all.

448
00:46:04,000 --> 00:46:09,000
 After the phone call, I should go over to them and say, "You know, I want to thank you.

449
00:46:09,000 --> 00:46:11,000
 I noticed that you let me have that phone call.

450
00:46:11,000 --> 00:46:16,000
 It was a very important phone call and you left me alone to make that phone call.

451
00:46:16,000 --> 00:46:17,000
 Thank you so much.

452
00:46:17,000 --> 00:46:22,000
 I just really respect that you had the ability not to do that."

453
00:46:22,000 --> 00:46:28,000
 And the 10-year-old is going to be very happy because you have basically given them positive reinforcement.

454
00:46:28,000 --> 00:46:30,000
 You've noticed their good behavior.

455
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:38,000
 But next time you make a phone call, they come over and they try to talk to you and it's not an emergency, so there's no real reason for them to be talking to you.

456
00:46:38,000 --> 00:46:41,000
 And when you get off the phone, you give them a demerit.

457
00:46:41,000 --> 00:46:49,000
 And let's say you've created a system that if they interrupt you five times, then they lose a half an hour of TV time.

458
00:46:49,000 --> 00:46:55,000
 And so they interrupted you twice during that phone call so they get two demerits.

459
00:46:55,000 --> 00:47:09,000
 After a while, if they begin to keep getting praise when they don't interrupt you, and they keep losing TV time when they do interrupt you, they're going to have the fastest change of behavior because they're going to learn that, "Hey, look at that.

460
00:47:09,000 --> 00:47:12,000
 When I do what's asked of me, good things begin to occur.

461
00:47:12,000 --> 00:47:16,000
 When I don't do what's asking me, bad things begin to occur."

462
00:47:16,000 --> 00:47:20,000
 But it's not so severe that I begin to avoid the punishment.

463
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:25,000
 I'm not going to completely not respond to you when you're on the phone.

464
00:47:25,000 --> 00:47:29,000
 You don't want that either because you want to be able to say, "Hey, Joe, come here and need to ask you a question.

465
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:31,000
 You want Joe to come to you.

466
00:47:31,000 --> 00:47:34,000
 You want Joe to come and tell you if there's a fire in the kitchen while you're on the phone."

467
00:47:34,000 --> 00:47:39,000
 So you want them to be able to use some common sense, so we need to use a little bit of both.

468
00:47:39,000 --> 00:47:42,000
 This works with dogs, this works with people, this works with everyone.

469
00:47:42,000 --> 00:47:47,000
 It's a combination of both reinforcement and punishment that gets the fastest change.

470
00:47:47,000 --> 00:47:51,000
 Punishment alone, we're just not going to see a lot of change.

471
00:47:51,000 --> 00:47:57,000
 Positive reinforcement alone, we may actually not see a lot of change either.

472
00:47:57,000 --> 00:48:03,000
 When we talk about reinforces, we also have to talk about different types of reinforces.

473
00:48:03,000 --> 00:48:05,000
 Primary reinforces are unlearned.

474
00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:08,000
 There are things that we naturally know, food, water, sex, pleasure.

475
00:48:08,000 --> 00:48:12,000
 We don't have to be taught that those are good things because we need those.

476
00:48:12,000 --> 00:48:14,000
 They're basic, they're biological.

477
00:48:14,000 --> 00:48:17,000
 And that's the big thing about this is that they need to be biological.

478
00:48:17,000 --> 00:48:21,000
 Secondary reinforcers, we've been taught have value.

479
00:48:21,000 --> 00:48:24,000
 Things like grades, approval, praise, money.

480
00:48:24,000 --> 00:48:26,000
 Money is an interesting one.

481
00:48:26,000 --> 00:48:28,000
 Money is actually what's called a token.

482
00:48:28,000 --> 00:48:30,000
 And it's what you say is tangible.

483
00:48:30,000 --> 00:48:33,000
 I can put my fingers on it, secondary reinforcer.

484
00:48:33,000 --> 00:48:38,000
 Now the reason that money is important to us is because we can exchange money for a primary

485
00:48:38,000 --> 00:48:39,000
 reinforcer.

486
00:48:39,000 --> 00:48:41,000
 I can take that money and I can buy food.

487
00:48:41,000 --> 00:48:44,000
 I can take that money and I can buy clothing.

488
00:48:44,000 --> 00:48:46,000
 I can take that money and I can even buy sex.

489
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:52,000
 I can take that money and I can exchange it for pleasure going to a movie in this case.

490
00:48:52,000 --> 00:48:57,000
 So it becomes important to me because it represents a primary reinforcer.

491
00:48:57,000 --> 00:48:59,000
 How do we know this?

492
00:48:59,000 --> 00:49:03,000
 The book has this picture of this chimpanzee, if you'll notice in it.

493
00:49:03,000 --> 00:49:06,000
 And he looks like he's over sort of at a slot machine.

494
00:49:06,000 --> 00:49:09,000
 What he's really over is at the chip-o-meter.

495
00:49:09,000 --> 00:49:13,000
 And basically what they did is they took some chimps and half the chimps, when they did

496
00:49:13,000 --> 00:49:17,000
 a specific behavior they wanted, they would give them a banana.

497
00:49:17,000 --> 00:49:21,000
 The other half the chimps, if they did the behavior, they got a token.

498
00:49:21,000 --> 00:49:27,000
 They then take that token and put it into the vending machine and bump out popped a banana.

499
00:49:27,000 --> 00:49:32,000
 The question was, would the chimpanzees work just as hard for that token, which represented

500
00:49:32,000 --> 00:49:35,000
 a banana, as they would if they actually got the banana?

501
00:49:35,000 --> 00:49:39,000
 And the truth was, is they worked just as hard for the chip.

502
00:49:39,000 --> 00:49:44,000
 In fact, in some cases the chips became even a little more valuable because I could kind of

503
00:49:44,000 --> 00:49:48,000
 weight cash in my chip a little bit later for when I really wanted my banana instead of having

504
00:49:48,000 --> 00:49:50,000
 the banana right now.

505
00:49:50,000 --> 00:49:55,000
 But either way, the token represented a primary reinforcer.

506
00:49:55,000 --> 00:49:59,000
 Now the thing about primary reinforcers is that they may be different.

507
00:49:59,000 --> 00:50:01,000
 Primary reinforcers are the same for everybody.

508
00:50:01,000 --> 00:50:05,000
 But secondary reinforcers and tokens may be different per person.

509
00:50:05,000 --> 00:50:10,000
 So I've got my American dollar, which is a good primary reinforcer, a secondary reinforcer

510
00:50:10,000 --> 00:50:12,000
 here for me because I can buy that primary reinforcer.

511
00:50:12,000 --> 00:50:15,000
 Sorry, I keep getting my two words mixed up.

512
00:50:15,000 --> 00:50:17,000
 But my token, let me just do this way.

513
00:50:17,000 --> 00:50:22,000
 My token of my dollar is important to me because I can go and exchange it for a soda.

514
00:50:22,000 --> 00:50:27,000
 But if I take that same US dollar and I go to Vietnam and I'm down in the middle of the

515
00:50:27,000 --> 00:50:34,000
 Mekong Delta, my token isn't very important anymore because I cannot exchange it for a

516
00:50:34,000 --> 00:50:35,000
 primary reinforcer.

517
00:50:35,000 --> 00:50:39,000
 My US dollar is useless to buying a Coca-Cola.

518
00:50:39,000 --> 00:50:42,000
 I'm assuming they have Coca-Cola, Mel, and Mekong Delta.

519
00:50:42,000 --> 00:50:47,000
 But I can't buy a drink with it because they want Vietnamese money, which is called the

520
00:50:47,000 --> 00:50:48,000
 Dong.

521
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:50,000
 So if I have Vietnamese Dong, sure, great.

522
00:50:50,000 --> 00:50:56,000
 I can now go get myself a soda, but an American dollar is useless.

523
00:50:56,000 --> 00:51:03,000
 So secondary reinforcers are only valuable if they can be exchanged or they can get me

524
00:51:03,000 --> 00:51:06,000
 something that is a primary reinforcer.

525
00:51:06,000 --> 00:51:11,000
 That's why those gold stars are so important to little kids because for them, those 10

526
00:51:11,000 --> 00:51:14,000
 gold stars equal the same as $10 bills to you and me.

527
00:51:14,000 --> 00:51:15,000
 I get 10 gold stars.

528
00:51:15,000 --> 00:51:17,000
 I get to go buy a toy.

529
00:51:17,000 --> 00:51:22,000
 So the star represents my ability to exchange for pleasure.

530
00:51:22,000 --> 00:51:29,000
 Before the end to this lecture, we will hit upon some more examples in class.

